# Quantum resource estimates for computing elliptic curve discrete logarithms

Martin Roetteler, Michael Naehrig, Krysta M. Svore, Kristin Lauter

Microsoft Research

ASIACRYPT 2017 / arXiv:1706.06752v3, 31 Oct 2017

## Abstract

We give precise quantum resource estimates for Shor's algorithm to compute
discrete logarithms on elliptic curves over prime fields. The estimates are
derived from a simulation of a Toffoli gate network for controlled elliptic
curve point addition, implemented within the framework of the quantum computing
software tool suite LIQUi\|). We determine circuit implementations for
reversible modular arithmetic, including modular addition, multiplication and
inversion, as well as reversible elliptic curve point addition. We conclude
that for computing elliptic curve discrete logarithms on a superconducting
qubit-based quantum computer, roughly \((448 \log_2 n + 4090) \cdot n^3\)
Toffoli gates are needed to solve the discrete logarithm problem on an
elliptic curve defined over a prime field of size \(n\) bits.

